From b4245e55c7f5d483a7a682968abec028883ea934 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:30:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Keep detached subagent observers until the last chat of
 the session closes

Detached subagent observers are shared by every chat of a backend session, but the
per-chat teardown disposed all of them. A session can have the default chat and one
or more peer chats open at once, so closing an unrelated peer chat tore down a
background observer owned by the still-open default chat, leaving the subagent's
later tool requests unclaimed again.

They now follow the same rule as the session-level input-needed watcher they feed:
release only once no sibling chat of that backend session is still active or
hydrating, tested through `_hasOtherSessionHold`. Scoping them per chat instead
would strand the subagent whenever its own chat closed, even though the watcher
that executes its tools outlives that chat.

The late-subagent test now exercises the path it names. It emits the child turn and
a client-contributed tool call after the parent turn has completed, and asserts the
detached observer processes them and claims the tool, rather than only asserting a
subscription reference still exists. The new peer-chat test asserts the whole path:
with both chats open, closing the peer must leave the subagent's client tool begun,
invoked once, completed, and attributed to the default chat that claimed it.
